{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"# Repair pushed — Gerrit change 2648\n\nRED-first, per the protocol. Plan-review PASSED and is certified (`verified_at_sha`\n`6c2267d8d7b1`) after four iterations; the ticket was claimed only after that.\n\n## RED evidence, captured before any fix existed\n\nThe mechanism test asserts observable behaviour — an argument-aware `journalctl` stub records what\nit was actually asked to emit — never source text:\n\n```\nFAILED test_steady_state_run_does_not_read_a_whole_copy_of_the_journal\n  AssertionError: one probe run read 48000 journal entries from a 4000-entry journal —\n  the probe re-reads the whole retained journal, once per counter\n\nFAILED test_journal_read_volume_does_not_scale_with_journal_size\n  AssertionError: read volume scaled with retained journal: 2400 entries read from a\n  200-entry journal, 96000 from a 8000-entry one\n```\n\n12.0x on both corpora, dead linear in retained-journal size. That is the confirmed mechanism\nreproduced as a test, and it is the number the RCA predicted.\n\nAlso RED before the fix: an unreadable journal published a fabricated `0`; an old-format state\nfile published a delta; a cursor journald no longer holds was left in place; both units lacked a\nstart timeout; and — behaviourally — a hung `git ls-remote` wedged the entire probe past 55 s.\n\nAll nine now pass, and the pre-existing suite (1443 tests in `tests/scripts`) passes with it.\n\n## What the fix is, and why it is scoped this way\n\n**The total is no longer recomputed, it is maintained.** Bounding the scan without changing the\ndesign was rejected explicitly: the delta was `recomputed_total - banked_total`, so a truncated\nscan yields a truncated total and a plausible WRONG number, and `--since` alone turns the total\ninto a window count the same subtraction renders meaningless. Each counter now persists a journald\ncursor beside its total and reads only what follows it. Read volume is a function of the interval,\nnot of retention.\n\nTwo contracts got stronger rather than merely preserved. A negative delta is no longer\nREPRESENTABLE, because nothing is subtracted — bug `2dc7`'s lost-history clamp becomes structural.\nAnd a historically inflated count (bug `8c2f`'s false positives) is inert, because the banked total\nis never an operand of the published value. Both suites were rewritten against the new mechanism\nrather than deleted.\n\n**A latent bug my own tests caught mid-flight, worth recording.** The first version returned the\ncount on stdout and the cursor in a global. Command substitution runs its body in a SUBSHELL, so\nthe cursor assignment was discarded and every run silently re-read from the same stale cursor. The\ndelta test caught it. The helper now returns both through globals, and the comment at that site\nsays why.\n\n**`TimeoutStartSec=240` on the observability unit, `90` on autodeploy**, both strictly below their\nperiods. Plus `Nice=10` + `IOSchedulingClass=idle` on the observability unit: it reads journald and\nwalks docker's storage on a box whose job is serving Gerrit, and it was competing at full priority\nwhile `rebar-autodeploy.service` already yielded.\n\n**Journald capping is NOT here**, per the scope-note comment: S3 owns it, and it would not have\nprevented this.\n\n## Step-7 sibling sweep — every hit has a disposition\n\nFixed in scope (same category, cheap, same file):\n\n| Site | Was | Now |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:58-62` IMDS token/region/instance-id curls | no bound; run FIRST, before any metric | `--max-time 5` |\n| `:1015` `git ls-remote` | no bound, a NETWORK read beside a correctly `--max-time 10` sibling | bounded 15 s |\n| `:452` debris `du` | unbounded stat walk over unplanned data-mount content | bounded |\n| `rebar-autodeploy.service` | `Type=oneshot`, no start timeout, `OnUnitActiveSec=2min` | `TimeoutStartSec=90` |\n\nFiled, not fixed — `arbitrary-allergenic-chameleon` (`a008-88cf-056e-44fb`), linked\n`discovered_from`: `autodeploy.sh:787`'s unbounded `git clone` (its `git fetch` sibling at `:798`\nIS bounded — the inconsistency-within-a-file signature again), and\n`install-certbot-timer.sh`'s `Type=oneshot` with no start timeout.\n\nExamined and deliberately NOT filed, so the sweep's coverage is legible: `bootstrap-gerrit-admin.sh`,\n`compose-up.sh`, `mcp-entrypoint.sh` also make unbounded curls and clones, but they are one-shot\nbootstrap and container-entry paths, not periodic units — a hang there is a failed boot, not a\nsilently missing metric.\n\n**`bounded` degrades to running the command directly where coreutils `timeout` is absent.** The\ncursor is what removes the unbounded read; refusing to run without `timeout` would take the metrics\noff the air on a host where nothing is wrong.\n\n## A test-suite finding that is part of the defect's history\n\nEvery `journalctl` stub in this suite was argument-BLIND — `cat \"$JOURNAL_FILE\"` — returning the\nwhole journal however it was asked for it. A stub that cannot tell a bounded request from an\nunbounded one cannot fail when the caller stops bounding, which is a large part of why twelve\nunbounded scans lived here for a month under 1443 passing tests. They now share a cursor-aware\nemulator that models rotation (`_journald_stub.py`), and the `timeout` stub is supplied explicitly\nrather than inherited, because coreutils `timeout` does not exist on macOS and the bound would\notherwise be exercised on Linux CI and silently skipped everywhere else.\n\n## Verification\n\n`make lint` and `make typecheck` exit 0. Ratchets shrink-only: complexity `new=0 increased=0`,\nmechanism-delta `new=0 increased=0` (the two new env vars carry `mechanism-ok:` markers).\n`tests/scripts`: 1443 passed, 3 skipped. `make verify` was NOT run — its concurrent load is\nimplicated in this outage; CI's `Verified +1` is the authoritative full-suite result.\n\nNothing was applied to the live host: no SSH, no SSM, no terraform apply. Terraform was not\ntouched at all, since `user_data.sh` left scope.\n\nChange 2648 is open and awaiting `LLM-Review` and `Verified`. **Not closing this ticket** — the\nchange has not landed, and the original AC1-AC3 (IOPS detection, memory alarming, heterogeneous\nadmission control) remain open detection work that this repair deliberately does not claim.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788571879086313326,"uuid":"97ae2c46-850f-440f-8398-e0519a5a62db"}